course: world
lesson: 170
title: The football argument that goes in circles
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Two boys argue: "Messi's better because he's the best." "No, Ronaldo's better because he's the greatest." For fifteen minutes. Each one just says his answer again in other words, so nothing moves. That is going in circles.
explain: Other tricks keep a playground argument spinning: throwing out the games you lost ("those don't count, the ref was bad"), making up a rule after you lose ("there's a base now"), and shouting louder ("In!" "Out!" "In!") when neither of you saw it.
explain: Some arguments are about a word, not a fact. Is a goalkeeper a "real footballer"? Is a hot dog a sandwich? If you don't agree what the word means, you can argue all day and nobody can win.
explain: The way out is to say what you count: goals, trophies, results, the league table. Check facts before using them: Messi has scored headers, including one in the 2009 Champions League final. And agree the rules before the game, not after.
explain: Kids mock the loop, not the fan: "Lads, 'best' and 'greatest' are the same word. You've been arguing with a thesaurus for fifteen minutes."
fact: Repeating your conclusion in other words as the reason is circular reasoning, or going in circles.
fact: Throwing out the results that go against you, or inventing a rule after you lose, is moving the goalposts.
fact: If you only count wins, every team in the world "always wins"; a fair count includes wins and losses.
fact: Saying something louder does not make it truer; if nobody saw clearly, agree a fair way to settle it, like a replay or a coin toss.
fact: Hoping ("it's our year") is not a reason; results, injuries and how good the other teams are give a better guess.
fact: Lionel Messi has scored headers, including one in the 2009 Champions League final against Manchester United.
fact: Check a fact before you argue with it: one false "fact" makes the whole argument wobble.
fact: Big clubs often have more fans because they come from big cities or won a lot long ago; who is better this season shows in the league table.
fact: Claims nobody can check, like "my dad's got a hundred black belts", are not real arguments.
fact: Goalkeepers are part of the football team, and the rules let them handle the ball in their own penalty area.
fact: Changing what a word means so that you win is a trap called "no true Scotsman"; arguing about a word nobody has defined is a verbal dispute.
fact: Asking "what do we each mean by the word?" often shows that both sides are right in their own way.
fact: Words people use: "lads" means boys or guys (UK), "a thesaurus" is a book of words that mean the same, "got cooked" means was beaten badly in an argument (young slang), "love that for you" is sarcastic, "the both of you" means both of you, and "the ref" is the referee.
exercise: "Messi's better because he's the best." "Ronaldo's better because he's the greatest." What is wrong? | Each says his answer again as the reason | They are both talking about the same player | One of them is right and the other wrong | They didn't argue for long enough
exercise: How could the Messi and Ronaldo argument become useful? | Say what you count: goals, assists, trophies | Shout louder and louder until one of them gives up | Ask everyone which player has the better haircut | Count how many fans each player has at the school
exercise: Jamal says his team always wins, but they lost the last three: "those don't count, the ref was bad." What is this trap? | Throwing out the games you lost | Counting all the results fairly | A verbal dispute about "win" | Shouting louder than the others
exercise: Lucas says his team is better because it has more fans, and has more fans because it's better. What is this? | Going in circles with popularity | A fair use of the league table | A fact about big cities | Moving the goalposts after a loss
exercise: Why do some clubs have more fans? | They come from big cities or won a lot long ago | They always play better than everyone | Fans only follow the best team each season | They pay people to be fans and to wear the shirts in the street
exercise: Two girls shout "In!" and "Out!" about a goal neither saw. What is the fair way to end it? | Agree a fair rule, like replaying the kick or a coin toss | Whoever shouts loudest wins | The older girl decides | Stop playing football forever
exercise: Ben says Arsenal will win the league "because it's our year", as he said the last two years. What is this? | Hope used as a reason | A careful look at the results | Circular reasoning about Arsenal | A fact checked in the league table
exercise: Noah says Messi can't head the ball. What is true? | Messi has scored headers, one in the 2009 Champions League final | Messi has never scored a header | Messi is not allowed to head the ball | Only goalkeepers score headers
exercise: In tag, a boy says "I was on base" when there's no base: "There is now." What is this? | Making up a rule after you lose | A fair rule agreed before the game | A verbal dispute about tag | A false fact about bases
exercise: Oscar and Ellie's dads get "a hundred black belts". Why is this not a real argument? | Nobody can check the claims and they just get bigger | Black belts can't be counted | Dads are not allowed to do karate | The taller dad always wins, so they only need to measure the dads
exercise: Freddie says goalkeepers aren't real footballers because they use their hands. What is true? | Goalkeepers are part of the team and may handle in their area | Goalkeepers are not in the football rules | Real footballers must never touch the ball | Goalkeepers may use their hands anywhere on the pitch, even in the other box
exercise: What trap is Freddie using with "real footballers"? | Changing a word's meaning to win, "no true Scotsman" | Hope used as a reason | Shouting louder than Molly | Counting only wins
exercise: Two kids argue for 20 minutes whether a hot dog is a sandwich. Why can't they settle it? | They don't agree what "sandwich" means | One of them has never even seen a hot dog | Hot dogs are definitely sandwiches, so one is lying | They need a proper referee to decide for them
exercise: What question helps end an argument that goes round and round? | "What do we each mean by the word?" | "Who is the loudest?" | "Whose dad is bigger?" | "Who said it first?"
exercise: What do grown-ups call repeating your conclusion as your reason? | Circular reasoning | Moving the goalposts | Wishful thinking | A verbal dispute
test: Girl nearby: "Lads, 'best' and 'greatest' are the same word. You've been arguing with a thesaurus for fifteen minutes." What is she mocking? | The circle, not the boys' taste in players | Messi for being small | Ronaldo for being tall | People who read books
test: His mate: "So the only games that count are the ones you win? Love that for you, bro." What would happen if every team counted like Jamal? | Every team in the world would "always win" | Only Jamal's team would always win | Nobody would ever win a match | The league table would be fair, because everyone would count the same way
test: Leo: "Round and round we go. You're a washing machine, Lucas." What does he mean? | Lucas's reason and claim keep pointing at each other | Lucas's kit needs washing | Lucas is good at spinning on the pitch | Lucas's team plays round in circles and never goes forward towards goal
test: Goalkeeper: "I was standing right there and even I don't know. You two were by the swings." What does this show? | Neither girl saw clearly, so shouting can't settle it | The goalkeeper is hiding the truth | The goal was definitely in | The goal was definitely out, so the goalkeeper should say so and end it
test: Uncle: "It's been your year for twenty years, lad. Your year's very patient." Is he insulting Ben? | No, he's teasing the reasoning, hope as a reason | Yes, he's calling Ben stupid | Yes, he hates Arsenal fans | No, he agrees with Ben that this really is Arsenal's year at last
test: Kai: "Champions League final 2009, header, look it up. You got cooked by a YouTube clip." What does "got cooked" mean? | Was badly beaten in the argument | Got really hungry during the match | Was badly burned by the sun outside | Won the whole argument really easily
test: Tagger: "The base is not real, the base is in your head." What would be fair? | Agree on a base before the next game, for everyone | Let the boy keep his base this time | Ban bases from all games forever | Let the tagger make up a new rule too, so it's even between them
test: Teacher: "Your dads are both accountants. The only fighting they do is with the printer." Which is the lesson? | Claims you can't check aren't arguments, and fights aren't worth bragging about | Accountants are secretly black belts | The taller dad would win a fight | Printers are dangerous to fight
test: Molly: "So by your rules the best goalie in the world isn't a footballer. Big brain move, Freddie." What does "big brain move" mean here? | A sarcastic way of saying the idea is silly | A real compliment for a clever idea that nobody had thought of before | A football tactic for goalkeepers | A move where you head the ball
test: Lunch lady: "You've spent all break on a sausage. Go outside, the both of you." What would actually settle the hot dog question? | Agreeing first what "sandwich" means | Asking the lunch lady to have a vote on it | Eating the hot dog quickly before it's cold | Arguing for another 20 minutes after school
test: A clever one: Sam says, "My team is the best, and if they lose, it's because they weren't really trying, so they're still the best." Can any result ever prove Sam wrong? | No, so his claim tells us nothing | Yes, if they go on and lose three games | Yes, if the referee agrees with Sam's view | No, because his team really is the best
exam: Identify the principal defect in the argument that a player is superior "because he is the best". | The conclusion is restated in place of a reason | The player's nationality is omitted | The argument relies too heavily upon statistics such as goals and trophies | The argument was made during a break
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Lionel Messi and headed goals. | He has scored headed goals, including one in the 2009 Champions League final | He has never scored a headed goal in any competition | Headed goals are prohibited for players of his height | He has scored exclusively with his left foot
exam: Determine the most appropriate method of resolving a dispute as to whether a ball crossed the line when no party observed it clearly. | Agreement upon a fair procedure, such as a replay or a coin toss | Determination in favour of the louder party | Determination in favour of the elder party | Indefinite continuation of the dispute until one party abandons the field
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a protracted dispute as to whether a hot dog is a sandwich? | A verbal dispute arising from an undefined term | A question to be settled by scientific measurement of the bread | A matter of established law | A case of collective punishment
